{
  "id": "binary-search-2d-row-sorted",
  "questionId": "binary-search-2d-row-sorted",
  "slug": "binary-search-2d-row-sorted",
  "title": "Search in a Row-Sorted Matrix",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "each row on its own is a sorted array - what's the fast way to check if a value is in a sorted array?",
    "since rows aren't sorted relative to each other, you can't rule out whole rows the way you could in a fully sorted matrix - you still need to check each row",
    "run a standard binary search inside every row, stopping as soon as one of them finds the target",
    "if you can bound target using a row's first and last element before searching it, you can skip rows that can't possibly contain it"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "assuming the matrix is fully sorted (like a flattened sorted array) and binary searching across rows, which is wrong since only individual rows are sorted here",
    "doing a full linear scan per row instead of binary search, missing the point of the pattern",
    "not handling a matrix with a single row or single column as a valid edge case"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a 2D grid of integers where every individual row is sorted in ascending order (rows have no relationship to each other beyond that), and a target value.\n\n**Determine whether the target appears anywhere in the grid.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= rows, cols <= 300",
      "-10^9 <= matrix[i][j] <= 10^9",
      "each row is individually sorted in ascending order",
      "-10^9 <= target <= 10^9"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "target smaller than every value in every row",
      "target larger than every value in every row",
      "a single row or a single column matrix"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "matrix = [[1,5,9],[10,11,13],[12,13,15]], target = 13",
        "output": "true",
        "expectedOutput": "true",
        "explanation": "13 appears in row 1 and again in row 2"
